'' OPEN's device forms, decided by whether the device word resolved to a variable.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/open-devices.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered

'' All six entry points, each with the ENCODING form's seven-argument signature.
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenCons(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenErr(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenScrn(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenPipe(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenLpt(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpenCom(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5, ptr $6)

'' ...AND the ordinary one, for the two spelled as variables. If the device rule matched
'' on the SPELLING rather than on whether the name bound, this would be absent.
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare i32 fb_FileOpen(ptr $0, i32 $1, i32 $2, i32 $3, i32 $4, i32 $5)

'' 'open err' must not reach the error-number reader. That was the miscompile.
'' EXPECT-NOT: fb_ErrorGetNum

'' The GOLDEN pins the ARGUMENTS -- the empty descriptor, each mode number, and the null
'' encoding -- which no substring assertion can state.
'' GOLDEN: expected/open-devices.out
